      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When implementing hardware security keys, you'll want to be aware of a few common technical hurdles that may arise during the login process. One of the most common issues is the need to update the key's QR code or NFC tag, as these codes can become outdated or corrupted over time. This can lead to users being unable to register their keys or authenticate with the system.\n\nAnother potential issue is the need to update the key's firmware, as new versions of the key's software may be released to improve security or fix bugs. This can cause problems if users are using an older version of the key, as they may not be able to update it properly.\n\nYou'll also want to consider the compatibility of the hardware security key with your organization's existing systems and applications. Some older systems may not support the use of hardware security keys, or may require additional configuration or software updates to work properly.\n\nIn terms of legacy software, you may need to update the software to support the use of hardware security keys. This could involve updating the software's authentication mechanisms, or adding new configuration options to support the use of the keys.\n\nIt's also worth noting that some users may experience issues with the key's pairing process, such as the key not registering properly with the",
